      ],
      "text/plain": [
       "           Count  Percentage\n",
       "Attrition                   \n",
       "0           1233       83.88\n",
       "1            237       16.12"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    },
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Missing values after target encoding: 0\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "y_encoded = y.map({\n",
    "    \"No\": 0,\n",
    "    \"Yes\": 1\n",
    "})\n",
    "\n",
    "target_encoding_summary = pd.DataFrame({\n",
    "    \"Count\": y_encoded.value_counts(),\n",
    "    \"Percentage\": (\n",
    "        y_encoded\n",
    "        .value_counts(normalize=True)\n",
    "        .mul(100)\n",
    "        .round(2)\n",
    "    )\n",
    "})\n",
    "\n",
    "display(target_encoding_summary)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\n",
    "    \"Missing values after target encoding:\",\n",
    "    y_encoded.isnull().sum()\n",
    ")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "d667a562",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 4. Numerical and Categorical Predictors\n",
    "\n",
    "Predictor variables are divided according to their data type because numerical\n",
    "and categorical attributes require different preprocessing operations.\n",
    "\n",
    "Several integer-valued predictors represent ordered categories, such as\n",
    "satisfaction levels, education level, job involvement, and work-life balance.\n",
    "\n",
    "These variables are kept in their original ordinal numerical representation,\n",
    "preserving the ordering defined in the dataset."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 19,
   "id": "edf04191",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Number of numerical predictors: 23\n",
      "['Age', 'DailyRate', 'DistanceFromHome', 'Education', 'EnvironmentSatisfaction', 'HourlyRate', 'JobInvolvement', 'JobLevel', 'JobSatisfaction', 'MonthlyIncome', 'MonthlyRate', 'NumCompaniesWorked', 'PercentSalaryHike', 'PerformanceRating', 'RelationshipSatisfaction', 'StockOptionLevel', 'TotalWorkingYears', 'TrainingTimesLastYear', 'WorkLifeBalance', 'YearsAtCompany', 'YearsInCurrentRole', 'YearsSinceLastPromotion', 'YearsWithCurrManager']\n",
      "\n",
      "Number of categorical predictors: 7\n",
      "['BusinessTravel', 'Department', 'EducationField', 'Gender', 'JobRole', 'MaritalStatus', 'OverTime']\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "numerical_features = X.select_dtypes(\n",
    "    include=np.number\n",
    ").columns.tolist()\n",
    "\n",
    "categorical_features = X.select_dtypes(\n",
    "    exclude=np.number\n",
    ").columns.tolist()\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f\"Number of numerical predictors: {len(numerical_features)}\")\n",
    "print(numerical_features)\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f\"\\nNumber of categorical predictors: {len(categorical_features)}\")\n",
    "print(categorical_features)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "7b26575d",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 5. Preprocessing Transformations\n",
    "\n",
    "Numerical predictors are standardized using `StandardScaler`.\n",
    "\n",
    "Standardization places numerical variables on comparable scales, which is\n",
    "especially important for algorithms based on distances or margins, such as\n",
    "K-NN and Support Vector Machines.\n",
    "\n",
    "Categorical predictors are transformed using one-hot encoding. This allows\n",
    "categorical information to be used by scikit-learn classifiers without\n",
    "introducing an artificial ordering between categories.\n",
    "\n",
    "Unknown categories are ignored during transformation so that the pipeline can\n",
    "process categories that may not have appeared in a particular training fold."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 20,
   "id": "73bd4a4f",
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "numeric_transformer = StandardScaler()\n",
    "\n",
    "categorical_transformer = OneHotEncoder(\n",
    "    handle_unknown=\"ignore\"\n",
    ")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "406940e9",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 6. Preprocessing Pipeline\n",
    "\n",
    "A `ColumnTransformer` is used to apply different preprocessing operations to\n",
    "numerical and categorical predictors.\n",
    "\n",
    "Numerical variables are standardized, while categorical variables are\n",
    "one-hot encoded.\n",
    "\n",
    "This transformer will later be included inside the complete machine-learning\n",
    "pipelines used during model evaluation."
   ]
  },
